# pytest & Python Conventions

## Python Style

- Modern syntax: `list[str]`, `X | None`, f-strings. Type-hint function signatures.
- Naming: `snake_case` functions/vars, `PascalCase` classes, `UPPER_SNAKE` constants.
- Keep functions short; use `@dataclass` for structured test data rather than loose dicts.

## Tests

- Name tests by behavior: `test_<action>_<expected_result>` (e.g. `test_login_with_locked_user_shows_error`).
- Use plain functions, not `unittest.TestCase`.
- One behavior per test, laid out as Arrange / Act / Assert. Tests must not depend on each other.
- Keep logic (`if`, loops, `try`) out of tests; move setup into fixtures and actions into Page Objects.

## Fixtures

- Put shared fixtures in `conftest.py`; never import them directly.
- Use `yield` for teardown. Keep the default `function` scope unless there's a clear reason not to.

## Parametrization & Markers

- Use `@pytest.mark.parametrize` with `ids=` so reports stay readable.
- Markers: `smoke`, `regression`. Register them in `pytest.ini`. `skip` always needs `reason=`.

```ini
[pytest]
testpaths = tests
addopts = --strict-markers -ra --base-url https://www.saucedemo.com
markers =
    smoke: critical path
    regression: full suite
```

```python
@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    ("username", "error"),
    [
        ("locked_out_user", "locked out"),
        ("", "Username is required"),
    ],
    ids=["locked_user", "empty_username"],
)
def test_login_with_invalid_user_shows_error(page: Page, username: str, error: str) -> None:
    login_page = LoginPage(page).open()
    login_page.login(username, "secret_sauce")
    expect(login_page.error).to_contain_text(error)
```
